//! Fortnite 1.8 Settings-screen UI materials (UE 4.16 Slate material brushes), ported from the cooked pixel shaders.
//!
//! GENERATED by tools/settings-ui-uimat-gen.cjs (run `node tools/settings-ui-materials.cjs`); do not edit by hand.
//! Pure Rust, no GL, no I/O: every `ps_*` function below IS a cooked D3D11 Slate material pixel shader translated instruction by instruction
//! (the compiled DXBC lives in the cooked MaterialInstance/Material .uexp; reports/settings-ui-shaders-1.8/<Material>/ holds the ASM and a
//! readable formula), every `cb_*` function builds the material constant buffer by evaluating the cooked FMaterialUniformExpressionSet with the
//! instance parameters (`PARAMS_*`, the instance's effective parameters from the cooked parent chain), and the few textures the shaders sample
//! are embedded as RGBA8.
//!
//! What Slate feeds the pixel shader (verified in the disassembled Slate vertex shader `FSlateElementVS` + the Slate material PS permutations):
//!  * `COLOR` (v1)   = brush tint x widget colour/opacity, packed to sRGB bytes by the batcher (`ToFColor(true)`) and decoded to LINEAR by the VS,
//!  * `TexCoord0` (v8) = `MaterialTexCoords` = LINEAR 0..1 over the element (the gradient/shine/icon parts of the materials rely on it; the Settings
//!    background Border-MainL has nonsensical nine-patch margins [0.1,0.45,0.9,0.5] which only make sense if material uv ignores them),
//!  * `v6` = `TextureCoordinates` = (u, v, tiling = 1, 1) with (u, v) = the brush uv: nine-patch mapped through the brush margin for Box brushes
//!    (this is what keeps the SDF corner radius / slanted tab edge at a fixed pixel size), linear for Image brushes,
//!  * `v7.zw` = element size in pixels (`PixelSize`; the Radio check icon uses IconSize / PixelSize), the clip-quad test (v3/v4) is dropped,
//!  * `cb0[2]` = Slate GammaAndAlphaValues = (2.2/DisplayGamma, 1/DisplayGamma): != 1 -> the shader applies `LinearToSrgb(pow(c, x))` itself,
//!  * `cb1[131].z` (View uniform buffer, only for materials with a Time node) = View time in seconds.
//! The output is therefore sRGB-encoded straight-alpha RGBA, exactly what the 4.16 non-sRGB backbuffer receives with SrcAlpha/InvSrcAlpha blending.
